✅ Basic Concept Questions
1.	Q: What is Prim’s Algorithm?
A: Prim’s Algorithm is a greedy algorithm that finds the Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) for a connected weighted graph. It starts from a vertex and continuously adds the smallest possible edge that connects a new vertex to the tree.
2.	Q: What is a Minimum Spanning Tree (MST)?
A: MST is a subset of the edges of a connected, weighted graph that connects all vertices with the minimum possible total edge weight and without any cycles.
3.	Q: What is the time complexity of Prim’s Algorithm?
A:
o	Using an adjacency matrix and a linear search (minKey): O(V²)
o	Using a priority queue with adjacency list: O(E log V) (more efficient)
________________________________________
✅ Code-Specific Questions
4.	Q: What is the purpose of the minKey() function?
A: It finds the index of the vertex with the minimum key value that has not yet been included in the MST (i.e., not in mstSet).
5.	Q: Why is sys.maxsize used in the key[] array?
A: It initializes the keys with a very large value (like infinity) to ensure any edge weight is smaller, allowing proper updates.
6.	Q: What do the key[], parent[], and mstSet[] arrays represent?
A:
o	key[]: Minimum weight edge to connect a vertex to the MST.
o	parent[]: Stores the parent node to print the MST later.
o	mstSet[]: Boolean array that tracks whether a vertex is included in the MST.
7.	Q: Why is the parent of vertex 0 set to -1?
A: Because vertex 0 is considered the starting/root node of the MST, and it has no parent.
8.	Q: How are edges represented in the graph?
A: The graph is represented using a 2D adjacency matrix, where graph[i][j] stores the weight of the edge between vertex i and j.
9.	Q: What condition checks whether to update the key of a vertex?
A:
10.	if self.graph[u][v] > 0 and mstSet[v] == False and key[v] > self.graph[u][v]
This checks that there is an edge (>0), the vertex is not yet in MST, and the current edge is smaller than the existing key.
